# Findings gitleaks should not fail on, by fingerprint
# (commit:path:rule:line). A fingerprint identifies a finding without
# repeating the value, which is why this file exists rather than an
# allowlist regex in .gitleaks.toml -- writing the string there would put it
# on main, and this repo's own pre_commit gate refuses that, correctly.
#
# Scope each entry to one finding. Never a bare path: that also silences
# every future rule on the file, which is how scanners quietly stop working.

# A fake AWS key in a test fixture on the closed #59 branch
# (.claude/hooks/stop-commit.test.sh:211). The test writes it to a temp file
# to prove the Stop-commit hook's gitleaks pass refuses the commit -- the
# string existing is the test working, not a leak. Never a real key.
#
# The scan covers full history across every ref, so this failed gitleaks on
# every PR, including ones whose diff never touched it. Allowlisted rather
# than removed because #59's branch still holds work to port onto main (the
# gitleaks pass itself, and --dry-run); deleting the ref would put those
# commits up for garbage collection. Drop this entry once #59 is ported and
# the branch is gone.
